1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,380
Your first fifteen seconds decide whether anyone stays to the end.

2
00:00:04,530 --> 00:00:09,670
Most openings fail because they summarise the video instead of asking a question.

3
00:00:09,820 --> 00:00:14,200
The viewer already knows the topic from the title they clicked.

4
00:00:14,350 --> 00:00:20,250
What they do not know is why this video is different from the last ten.

5
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:24,970
So answer that, and only that, before anything else.

6
00:00:25,120 --> 00:00:29,500
Confirm the title, open a gap, then prove the payoff exists.

7
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:36,660
Now the thumbnail, which most people design last and in a hurry.

8
00:00:36,810 --> 00:00:37,390
um

9
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:42,680
Shrink it to the size of a postage stamp before you choose it.

10
00:00:42,830 --> 00:00:44,550
Three words is the

11
00:00:44,700 --> 00:00:45,280
uh

12
00:00:45,430 --> 00:00:50,190
Three words is the most a thumbnail can carry at feed size.

13
00:00:50,340 --> 00:00:55,100
And it should never repeat the words already sitting in the title.

14
00:00:56,700 --> 00:01:01,080
Contrast matters more than colour when the image is that small.

15
00:01:03,980 --> 00:01:08,740
Retention is where the honest feedback lives, and almost nobody exports it.

16
00:01:08,890 --> 00:01:13,270
Open the audience retention chart in studio and download the file.

17
00:01:13,420 --> 00:01:14,380
so yeah

18
00:01:14,530 --> 00:01:20,050
Steep cliffs are moments where people left, usually a topic change with no signpost.

19
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:25,340
A slow slide is pacing, and the fix is cutting rather than rewriting.

20
00:01:25,490 --> 00:01:30,250
Fix the single biggest cliff first and ignore everything else this week.

21
00:01:31,250 --> 00:01:35,630
That is the whole system, and it fits on one page.

